  "blocks": [
    {
      "block_id": 0,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "NOTE\n",
      "bbox": [
        285.5,
        69.06505584716797,
        315.41448974609375,
        79.56505584716797
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.4751759930085324,
        0.08947641525920015,
        0.5249646002605242,
        0.10307956592196944
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 1,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The offset values of the registers are the addresses that the Copper must use to talk to \nthe registers.\n",
      "bbox": [
        110.75,
        88.26506805419922,
        492.8766174316406,
        111.21208953857422
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1843283405453414,
        0.11435076371436736,
        0.8203262210814934,
        0.14407950566805852
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 2,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "For example, in assembler:\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.0510025024414,
        126.31108856201172,
        203.52508544921875,
        136.81109619140625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.143220212131269,
        0.1636408350559468,
        0.33873987593863025,
        0.1772439956028878
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 3,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "INCLUDE\n\"exec/types.i\"\nINCLUDE\n’'hardware/custom, i\"\n",
      "bbox": [
        129.49899291992188,
        150.22491455078125,
        273.09814453125,
        168.57489013671875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.21553349406069627,
        0.1946221091367544,
        0.45453478814859694,
        0.21839520271329213
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 4,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "XREF\n_custom\n; External reference...\n",
      "bbox": [
        128.89903259277344,
        179.723876953125,
        423.915283203125,
        188.3739013671875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.2145349415415422,
        0.23283914056098765,
        0.7055494418477694,
        0.2440455772601424
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 5,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "Start: \nlea\ncustom,aO\n; Use aO as base register\nmove.w\n#$7FFF,intena(aO)\n; Disable all interupts\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.39901733398438,
        199.77288818359375,
        434.5318298339844,
        218.62261962890625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14379943557491218,
        0.25881339964742767,
        0.7232192425050022,
        0.2832339460096492
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 6,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "In C, you would use the structure definitions in hardware/customh For example:\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.04901123046875,
        234.15679931640625,
        438.3135986328125,
        244.65679931640625
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14321689792938824,
        0.3033590685536104,
        0.729513483290683,
        0.3169622192163797
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 7,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "#include\n\"exec/types.h\"\n#include\n\"hardware/custom.h\"\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.39900207519531,
        258.32061767578125,
        272.2176513671875,
        276.9206237792969
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.1437994101787292,
        0.33466421728983603,
        0.4530693268050921,
        0.35876123494265044
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 8,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "extern struct\nCustom custom;\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.04901123046875,
        287.9696350097656,
        250.1841278076172,
        296.4696350097656
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14321689792938824,
        0.3730756506038596,
        0.41639751791929275,
        0.38408772494991095
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 9,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "/* You may need to define the above external as\n** extern struct Custom far custom;\n** Check you compiler manual.\n*/\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.49898529052734,
        307.66864013671875,
        337.6271667480469,
        347.5018005371094
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14396581866779953,
        0.39859646342753663,
        0.56193458573086,
        0.45020184269427654
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 10,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "main ()\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.79902648925781,
        357.56561279296875,
        117.61451721191406,
        366.06561279296875
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14280083226339213,
        0.46323989549030314,
        0.19575339757754953,
        0.4742519698363545
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 11,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "{\ncustom.intena = 0x7FFF; \n/* Disable all interupts */\n}\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.14872741699219,
        366.8977966308594,
        402.3661193847656,
        397.2958068847656
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14338286198525618,
        0.4753300957531114,
        0.669683783998759,
        0.5147118779752345
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 12,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "The Amiga hardware include files are generally supplied with your compiler or assembler. List­\nings of the hardware include files may also be found in the Addison-Wesley Amiga ROM Kernel \nManual “ Includes and Autodocs” . Generally, the include file label names are very similar to the \nequivalent hardware register list names with the following typical differences.\n",
      "bbox": [
        85.2987289428711,
        411.39581298828125,
        517.8316650390625,
        460.6438293457031
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14196815491346557,
        0.5329789739154336,
        0.8618605101443021,
        0.5967816583299609
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 13,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "• \nAddress registers which have low word and high word components are generally listed as \ntwo word sized registers in the hardware register list, with each register name containing \neither a suffix or embedded “ L” or “ H” for low and high. The include file label for the \nsame register will generally treat the whole register as a longword (32 bit) register, and there­\nfore will not contain the “ L” or “ H” distinction.\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.0487289428711,
        475.9428405761719,
        517.5301513671875,
        538.1858520507812
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14321642810000285,
        0.6166021111641957,
        0.8613586815683342,
        0.6972403076207281
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 14,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "• \nRelated sequential registers which are given individual names with number suffixes in the \nhardware register list, are generally referenced from a single base register definition in the \ninclude files. For example, the color registers in the hardware list (COLOROO, COLOROl, \netc.) would be referenced from the “ color” label defined in “ hardware/custom.i” (color+0, \ncolor+2, etc.).\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.0487289428711,
        553.6858520507812,
        517.5301513671875,
        615.7798461914062
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14321642810000285,
        0.7173211490752922,
        0.8613586815683342,
        0.7977662878893909
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 15,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "• \nExamples of how to define the correct register offset can be found in the hw_examples.i file \nlisted in Appendix J.\n",
      "bbox": [
        86.2987289428711,
        630.6288452148438,
        517.5231323242188,
        654.1788330078125
      ],
      "bbox_norm": [
        0.14363251916218195,
        0.8170037327376894,
        0.8613469993241568,
        0.8475136405523687
      ]
    },
    {
      "block_id": 16,
      "type": 0,
      "text": "8 Introduction\n",
      "bbox": [
